2. Securely fasten seat belt and shoulder harness:
• Tighten firmly;
• Seat belt and harness straps must not be twisted; and
• When tightening the shoulder harness, make sure that the seat belt (lap strap) remains low across the hips and that the buckle is positioned correctly, as per manufacturer instructions;
3. Lean back and keep head against the backrest/headrest;
4. Cross arms in front of the chest (do not hold the shoulder harness straps);
5. Place feet and legs slightly apart;
6. Place feet flat on the floor; and
7. Keep knees bent at 90 degrees. Note — there are currently no studies that have used double cabin crewmember seats. Therefore, no recommendations have been made in this document.

The flight control surfaces are;
1. Electrically controlled
2. Hydraulically activated The stabilizer and rudder can be mechanically controlled. The side sticks are used to fly the aircraft in pitch and roll and indirectly through turn coordination in yaw. (pitch refers to the up or down movement of an aircraft’s nose. Roll refers to the tilting of an air- craft’s wings up or down)

In dealing with a Bomb Threat directed against an aircraft inflight, some or all the following actions shall be taken:
1. Evaluation of the threat;
2. Response and/or decision, including whether the response would be overt or covert;
3. Conducting of inflight search procedures;
4. Evaluation of the possible effects on pressurization or pressure equalization;
5. Establishment of the order of search, for example, public areas may be searched first;
6. Announcement of the threat by the PIC to the passengers (in such a manner as not to cause passengers to get panicked);
7. Arrangement of possible passenger participation in a search;
8. Establishment of emergency procedures, if a suspect explosive device is located; and
9. Determination of flight routing, and landing and disembarking procedures. When a warning is classified specific, the PIC will take action having due regard to the nature of the

Hijack situation in flight or on ground
1. Gain confidence of the hijacker. Appear to be sympathetic and friendly;
2. Inform the Flight Deck crew promptly.
3. Exercise due care, to ensure that the passengers do not become panic and/or exited. Switch “Seat belt” sign on to restrain passengers from getting involved.
4. Information obtained on the hijacker/s from time to time shall be promptly conveyed to the PIC each time it is obtained and his instructions shall be carried out. Information obtained on the status of the hijacker/s which shall include;
• Number of hijackers and their gender;
• Description of weapons / explosive devices etc.;
• Organization
• Language spoken;
• Demands;

10. Whether the Public Health Passenger Locator Forms were distributed to passengers two (2) rows in-front, back and either sides? 9.13.4 If the Passenger is suspected to be suffering from Communicable Disease, The CCMs shall;
1. If possible, separate sick passenger from others by 6 feet (or 3 rows) or move adjacent passen- gers without compromising flight safety or exposing additional passengers, by placing the sick passenger preferably near a toilet for his/her use;
2. This has to be done after a proper evaluation of the situation as relocating the sick passenger may enhance the risk of spread of disease to the other passengers. It is advisable to allow the sick passenger to remain in the same location if he/she does not have vomiting or diarrhoea which need frequent visit to the toilet.
3. Cover the nose and mouth of the patient with a medical face mask (if tolerated), if there are respiratory symptoms (e.g. coughing or sneezing.) if the mask cannot be tolerated, the sick pas-

or sneezing and to perform hand hygiene thereafter;
4. Provide the sick passenger with a plastic bag for disposing, used tissues and an air sickness bag, if experiencing nausea or vomiting;
5. Store soiled items (used tissues, face masks, linen, pillows, blankets, seat pocket items etc.) in a biohazard bag if available or in a plastic bag and labelling it “ Bio Hazard”;
6. Keep interaction with sick passenger as brief as possible. Designate one (or two if a sick passen- ger requires more assistance) CCM to interact with the sick passenger, preferably only the CCM who has already had contact with the passenger. The CCM or anyone in direct contact with the sick passenger shall be using the Universal Precautionary Kit. The CCM/S should always wear gloves and perform hand hygiene after removing them.
7. Keep PIC informed of the necessity to immediate isolation of the passenger upon arrival;
8. Inform/ contact the Medical Officer of Health (MOH) immediately if CCM/s develop symptoms

